1
00:00:00,494 --> 00:00:04,612
Now, in physics, a quantum leap is a very small jump between two energy levels.

2
00:00:05,070 --> 00:00:07,652
In everyday speech, it means a very big one.

3
00:00:08,109 --> 00:00:11,280
Now, the word has been promoted without anyone asking it.

4
00:00:11,566 --> 00:00:15,920
India would like, of course, the promotion to apply to well the country as well.

5
00:00:16,398 --> 00:00:22,880
MIT's new quantum index report ranks India third in the world for research papers in both quantum computing and quantum communications.

6
00:00:23,054 --> 00:00:29,520
Patent filings from India went from 226 in 2023 to 2883 in 2025.

7
00:00:29,742 --> 00:00:30,980
Those are very real numbers.

8
00:00:31,438 --> 00:00:43,364
And they honestly reside next to a far less comfortable truth, which is count how often that research lands amongst the world's most cited papers, and India is fifth in computing and ninth in communications.

9
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:55,268
So, apart from computing, we are also quantum computing, we are also going to be talking to a gentleman who is a luminary when it comes to semiconductors and the nanotechnology aspect of things.

10
00:00:55,694 --> 00:00:58,000
I'm talking about the one and only Mr. Professor V.

11
00:00:58,350 --> 00:01:03,396
Ram Gopal Rao, who is the group vice-chancellor of Bit Spilani and former director of IIT Delhi.

12
00:01:03,854 --> 00:01:06,400
Professor Rao has led Bitspilani since 2023.

13
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:10,148
He was director of IIT Delhi from 2016 to 2021.

14
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:19,684
He is a nanoelectronics researcher with more than 50 patents, and he co-founded two deep technology startups at IIT Bombay.

15
00:01:20,110 --> 00:01:26,788
Now among his honours are the Shanti Swarup Bhatnagar Prize and the Infosys Prize as well.

16
00:01:27,214 --> 00:01:33,316
Professor, it's truly, truly an honor to have you exclusively with us on the front page.

17
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:35,716
Thank you so much for inviting me.

18
00:01:36,238 --> 00:01:38,160
It's my pleasure to speak with you today.

19
00:01:38,606 --> 00:01:39,524
Truly an honor, sir.

20
00:01:39,854 --> 00:01:54,960
Actually, let's start with the aspect of the entire semiconductor policy, which has been the recent motivation for going ahead and establishing our own manufacturing in our country.

21
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:58,020
We started off with assembly and OSATS, sir.

22
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:14,720
And that I believe, according to Mr. Chandak, when I was talking to him on front page, he said that that was deliberate because it was in a way trying to establish trust across the world that India can do it and then logically move towards the manufacturing aspect of things.

23
00:02:14,926 --> 00:02:22,340
Now, Semiconductor 2.0 has also talked about 1,27,500 crore rupees which have been allocated, sir.

24
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:29,444
Do you feel that we are moving in the right direction when it comes to semiconductors in India?

25
00:02:30,638 --> 00:02:32,160
I particularly think so.

26
00:02:32,334 --> 00:02:39,652
In fact, in my career in India, I came back to India from the US in 1998.

27
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:52,356
So in the last 26 years, I have seen twice such attempts to establish or get the semiconductor foundries to command a set of facilities in India, but did not succeed.

28
00:02:52,654 --> 00:02:54,692
I think third time we got lucky.

29
00:02:54,958 --> 00:02:57,360
I'm very happy that it's finally happening.

30
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:10,148
There are at least a dozen FAPs now which are coming up of different types, you know, some for ATMP, some for fabrication, some for you know various other kinds of components.

31
00:03:10,560 --> 00:03:28,240
But I think this is very important that you know India is finally able to manufacture some of these advanced technologies within the country, which I will explain to you why it is important because we have all been you know, doing research in the nano electronics kind of an area.

32
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:34,564
But this lack of facilities in India has always been a major concern for many of us.

33
00:03:35,758 --> 00:03:42,272
Professor, actually, I would like for you to go ahead and maybe shed light as you just mentioned in terms of the areas.

34
00:03:43,232 --> 00:03:48,164
and given your very, very astute and deep experience as far as the field is concerned.

35
00:03:48,558 --> 00:03:49,392
How do you view it?

36
00:03:49,472 --> 00:03:50,276
Because you know what?

37
00:03:50,702 --> 00:03:58,352
There is always a tendency in terms of the critiques who are in the environment to constantly compare ourselves with TSMC.

38
00:03:58,592 --> 00:04:14,912
We keep going ahead and listening to those constant barbs about the fact that, you know, India is far behind and the fact that, you know, it'll take an X amount of time and a huge amount of money for us to even start to think about well, maybe two nanometer or four nanometer or five nanometer.

39
00:04:15,438 --> 00:04:17,791
How would you like to address that thought?

40
00:04:17,952 --> 00:04:23,588
Because in my head, we need to start somewhere, and I think we have taken a good leap of faith.

41
00:04:24,832 --> 00:04:26,180
I totally agree with you.

42
00:04:26,446 --> 00:04:34,116
I think you know, just to answer that question, we need to look at China's journey, China has been at it for almost like 20 years.

43
00:04:34,592 --> 00:04:41,284
India is starting now, but China has been at it for almost like 20 years, And what has changed in China?

44
00:04:41,550 --> 00:04:50,992
You know, China was also not manufacturing, but not such a manufacturing hub for such high technologies earlier, was also struggling, like in India.

45
00:04:51,072 --> 00:04:54,500
There was a time when India and China were at the same level, right?

46
00:04:54,734 --> 00:04:59,472
But China started to invest in their universities, started to invest in manufacturing,

47
00:04:59,472 --> 00:04:59,792
right?

48
00:04:59,872 --> 00:05:14,192
Whoever wanted to manufacture something in the country, anything for that matter, from you know cigarette lighters to advanced chips, anything what they wanted, they said, you know, come manufacture in China, and we are fine with it, kind of thing.

49
00:05:14,272 --> 00:05:21,732
They provided the manpower, the skills required and the facilities required, the infrastructure, and then people started manufacturing.

50
00:05:22,030 --> 00:05:26,032
So once they were able to adapt to those technologies, right?

51
00:05:26,112 --> 00:05:28,900
They got them to come to China and do that.

52
00:05:29,072 --> 00:05:41,792
In the process, they they themselves started to understand these technologies, learn how these things are done, and then finally captured the entire market of actually manufacturing, right?

53
00:05:42,030 --> 00:05:52,740
What they got as a technology, as a knowledge earlier, I think with their sort of a you know mission mode, sort of an approach over a period of time, they were able to do everything themselves.

54
00:05:52,912 --> 00:06:00,032
And then it came to a point, which is where we are right now, where others have completely lost out on manufacturing.

55
00:06:00,192 --> 00:06:02,352
Now, without China, they cannot even do anything.

56
00:06:03,072 --> 00:06:09,732
once that China is able to do that, now they are applying all of that knowledge into actually doing new things.

57
00:06:09,998 --> 00:06:19,472
Now, a lot of new technologies, the fact that they are beginning to compete with the US, at the scale that US is doing things, right?

58
00:06:19,632 --> 00:06:37,892
At the quality that US is able to do things, the fact that President Trump was in China, so many times and then treating China so differently is because of that capability that China has established over almost like 20 years, which started as a very low-level manufacturing.

59
00:06:38,112 --> 00:06:48,832
But once they started to adapt to these technologies, you know, now they started applying these technologies to multiple new domains that now they are a major player in the world.

60
00:06:48,992 --> 00:06:52,272
I think for India, like you said, we have to start somewhere.

61
00:06:52,512 --> 00:06:53,892
I think this is a good beginning.

62
00:06:54,126 --> 00:07:05,312
And once these companies come and start manufacturing in India, I think the next step is for us to learn all of these advanced technologies because you know we have to like we have to start somewhere.

63
00:07:05,518 --> 00:07:08,432
We have to learn some of these by doing it ourselves.

64
00:07:08,592 --> 00:07:18,724
And I think sooner or later, we will be able to absorb them and we will be able to move ahead to developing new applications, new technologies, using this as a baseline.

65
00:07:18,926 --> 00:07:25,152
I think what has happened to China 20 years ago is beginning to happen in India now for the past few years.

66
00:07:25,232 --> 00:07:26,912
And I think we should welcome that.

67
00:07:26,992 --> 00:07:32,352
And you cannot reach that pinnacle of these technologies overnight, not possible.

68
00:07:32,558 --> 00:07:34,512
Nobody is going to give them to you on a platter.

69
00:07:35,232 --> 00:07:42,592
I think we have to provide subsidies and we have to provide them whatever that is required so they will come and manufacture in India.

70
00:07:42,798 --> 00:07:50,436
But the process going forward must be to absorb these technologies and then apply them to developing new technologies on our own.

71
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:52,452
Fair point, sir.

72
00:07:52,718 --> 00:08:05,488
I agree with you absolutely because I mean the start has to be somewhere, and I feel it's been a very positive start because I mean, finally, somewhere we are trying to take up ownership when it comes to something as crucial and critical as semiconductors and nanotechnologies.

73
00:08:05,806 --> 00:08:07,168
Which then brings me to this, sir.

74
00:08:07,328 --> 00:08:26,048
Could you be kind enough to maybe give us a sneak peek into what is generally the overall sort of a landscape in terms of how we are approaching the entire manner of manufacturing, assembly, testing, etc.?

75
00:08:26,288 --> 00:08:28,196
It had to be motivated from somewhere.

76
00:08:28,768 --> 00:08:31,648
As you mentioned, China is a particular example.

77
00:08:31,888 --> 00:08:41,727
Now, is it that we are trying to go ahead and say, okay, we need to become independent as far as up to, let's say, 128 nanometers are concerned?

78
00:08:41,888 --> 00:08:42,628
Because you know what?

79
00:08:42,847 --> 00:08:44,207
The volume is very high.

80
00:08:44,368 --> 00:08:45,568
We don't want to be dependent.

81
00:08:45,648 --> 00:08:47,716
We'd rather start manufacturing on our own.

82
00:08:48,048 --> 00:08:51,684
And then maybe the value isn't that great, but at least the volume is there.

83
00:08:51,888 --> 00:09:04,900
And then as we keep getting better and better, we start moving towards the more crucial, the very, very highly accurate chips, or maybe a four and a five or a six or a seven, etc., and things like that.

84
00:09:05,328 --> 00:09:09,888
Is that what has been at the center of the policy making and the decision making?

85
00:09:10,094 --> 00:09:13,668
Or is it something else which is far more far-sighted?

86
00:09:14,734 --> 00:09:15,300
I think so.

87
00:09:15,568 --> 00:09:22,128
In fact, if you look at some of the foundries that are coming up, now we are going down to 28 nanometer level.

88
00:09:22,208 --> 00:09:22,368
Correct.

89
00:09:22,528 --> 00:09:35,876
And 28 nanometer is a technology node where a lot of applications exist, if you are trying to develop an IOT kind of a system, You don't really need two nanometer, four nanometer kind of chip.

90
00:09:36,528 --> 00:09:52,688
for an advanced mobile phone or for an Nvidia kind of a chip, which is the ultimate level of you know perfection in these technologies, that we may not be able to do for the next 10 years, And that is a very specialized expertise, and nobody is going to give it to us so soon.

91
00:09:52,910 --> 00:10:01,488
But I think with 28 nanometer node technology, India can do so many things, you know, which otherwise we we would not even have ventured into.

92
00:10:01,648 --> 00:10:04,528
For example, you know, we are all talking of AI, right?

93
00:10:04,928 --> 00:10:11,328
When you talk about AI, any physical AI, right, will have to gather information from the surroundings.

94
00:10:12,174 --> 00:10:16,768
And now for it to gather information from the surroundings, it requires tons of sensors.

95
00:10:16,848 --> 00:10:26,848
It will require so many sensors to be integrated that it knows it where it is, it knows its position, it knows what is around it, it knows what action to take, right?

96
00:10:26,928 --> 00:10:36,528
That sensors and actuators kind of technologies are going to be crucial for any AI to succeed because that is how these systems become intelligent.

97
00:10:36,782 --> 00:10:42,608
Now, when you are talking about sensors, right, you are not talking of two nanometer, three nanometer, or anything like that.

98
00:10:42,798 --> 00:10:46,288
Here it is all based on pure chemistry, pure physics.

99
00:10:46,368 --> 00:10:53,700
You know, these technology nodes for making an entire IOT chip, you know, you can do all of that using 28 nanometer node.

100
00:10:53,888 --> 00:11:03,044
Just to give you an example, there were surveys where they said the country will require, not the country, the world will require something like a trillion sensors per year.

101
00:11:03,278 --> 00:11:06,948
One trillion sensors per year will be the market for IOTs.

102
00:11:07,630 --> 00:11:18,500
Now, even if you take one per sensor, you are talking of a trillion dollar market right in the sensors domain, And those kinds of technologies, India needs to become a pioneer.

103
00:11:18,768 --> 00:11:29,408
For developing sensor kind of technologies, you need basic sciences, you need chemistry, you need physics, you need electrical engineering, that we have in in good amount.

104
00:11:29,488 --> 00:11:38,368
And we have excellent chemists, we have excellent physicists, we have excellent engineers who can build a system together, all of that integrated with a chip, right?

105
00:11:38,528 --> 00:11:46,128
Which can communicate with the external world, which can make a humanoid to perceive information around it, right?

106
00:11:46,382 --> 00:11:57,248
I mean, that kind of a technology, India has a huge opportunity to get into IOTs, to get into manufacturing sensors and become a hub for the world when it comes to the sensing platforms.

107
00:11:57,454 --> 00:11:59,408
I think India has everything going for it.

108
00:12:00,064 --> 00:12:02,596
These are the areas that we need to focus on.

109
00:12:03,424 --> 00:12:06,304
and I think you know there are enormous opportunities.

110
00:12:06,464 --> 00:12:19,524
You don't have to always, you know, when you talk about advanced technologies, we don't have to talk about you know making a chip that is going into the next generation Apple phone or a Samsung phone or an Nvidia kind of a chip.

111
00:12:19,824 --> 00:12:32,224
There are, you know, there are trillions of dollars of market available for you know the sensors and communication systems, you know, the space technologies, which is again going to be a frontier kind of a science.

112
00:12:32,384 --> 00:12:37,156
You know, those technologies don't really need that advanced kind of technology nodes.

113
00:12:37,424 --> 00:12:38,384
I think defense, right?

114
00:12:38,544 --> 00:12:42,544
Defense is going to be now with the kind of turmoil the world is in, right?

115
00:12:42,734 --> 00:12:46,564
The defense technologies are going to be of utmost importance now.

116
00:12:46,704 --> 00:12:48,784
And they're, you know, it's all strategic.

117
00:12:48,944 --> 00:12:51,844
No one is going to sell you now any of these technologies.

118
00:12:52,174 --> 00:12:57,584
Whereas India can become a great exporter of these technologies and their reliability matters.

119
00:12:57,678 --> 00:13:09,184
And in that kind of a scenario, you don't want to go to a technology node, you know, which which could be very advanced, but which may not be reliable to the extent that is required for a missile.

120
00:13:09,390 --> 00:13:17,956
I think developing those seeker technologies and developing those missiles, I think all of that can be done at the 28-nanometer technology node.

121
00:13:18,144 --> 00:13:36,384
It is not to say that we should not go up further to a further advanced kind of nodes, but I think we have to stabilize at a node and capture some of the markets that exist for that node and then learn these technologies, adapt these technologies, and then move ahead to apply them.

122
00:13:36,590 --> 00:13:41,264
you know, for a variety of applications that are today not possible.

123
00:13:41,344 --> 00:13:41,664
today.

124
00:13:41,744 --> 00:13:48,420
You know, if you want to develop any of these technologies that have a strategic importance, nobody is going to manufacture it for you.

125
00:13:49,582 --> 00:13:55,652
I think there is a great opportunity for India to be establishing some of these nodes in the country today.

126
00:13:56,846 --> 00:13:58,704
Professor, I want to understand this from you.

127
00:13:58,864 --> 00:14:03,744
I mean, yes, we've talked about defense, you've talked about, you know, there being a great opportunity.

128
00:14:03,904 --> 00:14:05,668
More importantly, also sovereignty.

129
00:14:05,934 --> 00:14:11,584
We don't want anyone just suddenly twisting our arm uh, you know, when the going gets tough.

130
00:14:11,664 --> 00:14:20,944
So, I mean, I'm pretty sure those could be also the motivations behind, probably constructing a policy in terms of ensuring that.

131
00:14:21,024 --> 00:14:22,784
all right, we're going to tap into these areas.

132
00:14:22,990 --> 00:14:23,344
phase one.

133
00:14:23,504 --> 00:14:25,660
phase two, phase three, etc . etc.

134
00:14:25,998 --> 00:14:36,804
How important do you feel, sir, for somebody as brilliant and as awesome, a champion as yourself from academia to be a part of the entire policy making?

135
00:14:37,006 --> 00:14:38,384
uh methodology goes?

136
00:14:38,464 --> 00:14:42,784
The reason I'm asking you this is because, I mean, this is my personal observation.

137
00:14:42,864 --> 00:14:46,308
A lot of times the policies tend to kind of take place in silos.

138
00:14:46,702 --> 00:15:00,964
They're taken place or they're made out with people who might not have the extensive sort of inexperience to go ahead and chart out a policy, and then of course there are various amendments that keep coming into the policies as we keep moving along.

139
00:15:01,264 --> 00:15:07,264
But do you feel that we've been able to repair that method as far as the semiconductor policy is concerned?

140
00:15:07,470 --> 00:15:13,252
Is there enough presence of academia as well and industry as well in the policy making?

141
00:15:14,862 --> 00:15:24,004
I think you know, when you talk about policy in India, it's all the bureaucrats who are at the forefront of making these policies.

142
00:15:24,238 --> 00:15:34,544
And they are not specialists, you know, they are generalists, and they consult here and there and get some foreign experts and hire some of these agencies to do that.

143
00:15:34,704 --> 00:15:39,104
We all have you know their own personal interests in all of that.

144
00:15:39,310 --> 00:15:47,664
I think the involvement of academia in much of this policy making, I would expect I would want uh you know to improve.

145
00:15:47,744 --> 00:15:48,464
I think uh

146
00:15:48,464 --> 00:15:51,504
it's still not there, according to you, as much as it should be.

147
00:15:52,176 --> 00:15:54,576
not as much as I would have liked it, right?

148
00:15:54,656 --> 00:16:00,336
When you, for example, you know, you set up now these manufacturing facilities, right?

149
00:16:00,576 --> 00:16:02,116
We provide huge subsidies.

150
00:16:02,416 --> 00:16:07,696
These companies, because of subsidies, will come and start manufacturing things, What happens?

151
00:16:07,776 --> 00:16:12,576
Now, what you were buying earlier from TSMC, now you will buy it from a local foundry.

152
00:16:13,454 --> 00:16:14,948
That's the only thing that will change.

153
00:16:15,456 --> 00:16:21,296
but if we are not understanding these technologies, we are not getting involved in these technologies.

154
00:16:21,550 --> 00:16:30,020
If that learning is not happening in our academic institutions because of whatever is happening in these foundries, then nothing would have changed.

155
00:16:30,656 --> 00:16:35,376
The next generation will again, instead of importing, you are buying from a local vendor.

156
00:16:35,776 --> 00:16:45,136
But nothing would have changed in terms of doing anything new, That is where academia needs to get involved in a much stronger fashion.

157
00:16:45,390 --> 00:16:56,416
For example, if you look at China, some of their universities are now so ahead, The funding their universities are receiving for these advanced technologies is orders of magnitude.

158
00:16:56,576 --> 00:17:09,455
I was recently looking at you know the data, what China spends on two universities, you know, Xinghua University, Peking University, just two universities, is the entire Ministry of Education budget, right?

159
00:17:09,935 --> 00:17:14,496
What China spends on two universities is our entire Ministry of Education budget.

160
00:17:14,733 --> 00:17:21,215
So with those kind of budgets, you know, expecting that we will now learn these deep technologies, right?

161
00:17:21,296 --> 00:17:25,136
For you to you know, learn these technologies, you have to go and use them.

162
00:17:25,358 --> 00:17:33,216
You have to go and trial with that, you need to go and build something, you need to fail and learn from those failures and then you know develop further.

163
00:17:33,296 --> 00:17:34,852
But all of that needs to happen.

164
00:17:35,086 --> 00:17:41,776
But I think in the absence of such technologies, you know, getting established in all top academic institutions, right?

165
00:17:42,062 --> 00:17:52,976
I think you know sometimes our ability to learn anything new, ability to teach anything new for the next generation of children, also becomes a bit of a limiting factor, right?

166
00:17:53,166 --> 00:17:59,296
And we have all been working with government closely on that, particularly Ministry of Electronics and Information Technology.

167
00:17:59,456 --> 00:17:59,856
you know,

168
00:17:59,856 --> 00:18:00,816
has been, you know.

169
00:18:00,976 --> 00:18:05,856
I have been involved with the nanoelectronics kind of infrastructure building.

170
00:18:06,030 --> 00:18:08,196
you know, for the past, almost like 15 years.

171
00:18:08,496 --> 00:18:14,948
thanks to, you know, some of the some of the progressive secretaries we had in the ministry and leadership.

172
00:18:15,054 --> 00:18:20,964
we had, you know, at least there are seven institutions in India, which have good state-of-art facilities.

173
00:18:21,742 --> 00:18:26,052
But then we need them in many more institutions because India is a large country.

174
00:18:26,670 --> 00:18:36,336
And even the funding that these seven top IITs and IASC have received is also not at the same level as what one would have liked to see,

175
00:18:36,336 --> 00:18:36,736
right?

176
00:18:37,216 --> 00:18:44,896
India needs to invest more in academia, and then create more opportunities for academia to collaborate with.

177
00:18:45,070 --> 00:18:58,496
you know, all that that is happening as part of these missions, so that that learning will happen through academia, and then academia will be able to train the next generation of learners, and they will go on to build things which currently are not possible.

178
00:18:58,656 --> 00:19:01,856
For example, when I started my company, NanoSniffer, right?

179
00:19:02,016 --> 00:19:08,708
Nanosniff is a company we incubated in 2011 at IIT Bombay, where I was a faculty member.

180
00:19:08,896 --> 00:19:14,052
And then the whole technology was to detect explosives at the airports.

181
00:19:14,496 --> 00:19:26,896
And now it took us almost like 11 years to launch that product, and now there are 90 airports in India which are using nanosniffers after the security checkpoint.

182
00:19:27,086 --> 00:19:33,056
You know, when you look for where the security agencies actually scan boarding passes and take a swipe and do that.

183
00:19:33,262 --> 00:19:43,296
Now, whatever we were buying from companies abroad, now they are all replaced by indigenously developed technology, protected world over, right?

184
00:19:43,456 --> 00:19:46,052
Both in US and Europe, we own the patents.

185
00:19:46,176 --> 00:20:03,536
It's the world's first microsensor-based explosive detector, the world's first microsensor-based explosive detector, completely manufactured ingeniously and developed by NanoSniff, the company who had incubated all credit to the Nanosniff for doing that.

186
00:20:03,696 --> 00:20:10,336
But for them to go from a laboratory prototype to an airport, took them full 11 years, right?

187
00:20:10,542 --> 00:20:16,576
Of which at least five years we lost in finding facilities where we can fabricate in India.

188
00:20:16,736 --> 00:20:22,576
Because you cannot send out that IT abroad, to an agency which will manufacture things.

189
00:20:22,766 --> 00:20:37,696
First of all, they were charging a huge amount of money, which a startup at that, you know, in those times, at least I'm talking of 10 years ago, was not uh you know able to raise, you know, that you can send uh get these wafers processed of your intellectual property.

190
00:20:38,016 --> 00:20:42,756
So I mean, let me add to what you're just saying, it also doesn't make business commercial sense.

191
00:20:43,118 --> 00:20:47,396
Even if you're flooded with money, it doesn't make business commercial sense, no.

192
00:20:48,142 --> 00:20:51,408
So for that, we had to set up prototype manufacturing facilities.

193
00:20:51,568 --> 00:20:57,188
We had to establish them and then writing proposals, defending them, all of that took us full five years.

194
00:20:57,550 --> 00:21:14,608
Now, by the time you know we were ready with the proposals, ready with the facilities, no, we lost what could have been done in five to seven years, took us almost 11 years, And because we had a very unique technology and all that, we could still survive, We could raise money from other kinds of sources.

195
00:21:15,088 --> 00:21:16,528
but you know, that is what happens.

196
00:21:16,608 --> 00:21:24,356
In these kind of areas, doing research, you know, you can do with whatever basic facilities that that we have in the country.

197
00:21:24,558 --> 00:21:30,128
But if you want to develop advanced applications, it calls for different level of facilities.

198
00:21:30,350 --> 00:21:38,308
You may have a nanoelectronics foundry, but that is used by, let's say, 200 PhD students for doing 50 different things.

199
00:21:38,670 --> 00:21:46,288
Now, that kind of a foundry, which is so general, cannot be used to build a specific product, you know, which you can sell in the market.

200
00:21:46,448 --> 00:21:52,448
Because for selling something in the market and using these technologies, you have to meet a lot of requirements.

201
00:21:52,768 --> 00:21:55,728
The quality requirements are very stringent, right?

202
00:21:55,950 --> 00:22:02,980
And with 200 PhD students using it for 50 different projects, you will never be able to meet the quality requirements.

203
00:22:03,328 --> 00:22:10,048
And there are no dedicated facilities in the country for manufacturing any of these kinds of technologies today, right?

204
00:22:10,318 --> 00:22:21,728
So therefore, you know, anybody trying to start a company, right, you know, will find it very challenging because establishing a foundry would be easily a 30 crore or 40 crore, you know, kind of requirement for the startup.

205
00:22:21,966 --> 00:22:24,848
Now, how many startups in India can raise that kind of money?

206
00:22:25,328 --> 00:22:28,048
And how many investors are there to back it up, also, sir?

207
00:22:28,128 --> 00:22:31,300
That's another discussion which I want to have with you as we move along.

208
00:22:32,048 --> 00:22:38,288
So 11 years, what it took us to take a deep technology product to market, right?

209
00:22:38,928 --> 00:22:46,048
but then you know, the kind of money that we needed was never available, the kind of facilities that we required were not available.

210
00:22:46,350 --> 00:22:49,988
So everything was a struggle at every stage for the nanosniff company.

211
00:22:50,158 --> 00:22:56,128
But finally, you know, they were able to succeed because, of course, somebody in the government understood our challenge.

212
00:22:56,238 --> 00:23:00,528
Then in the Mighty, they were able to set up these facilities which we could use.

213
00:23:00,768 --> 00:23:08,288
But the only place where we could fabricate these chips today is still in IAC Bangalore, an academic place, right?

214
00:23:08,494 --> 00:23:17,924
And there is no other foundry in the country which can manufacture what is going into that nanosniffer, which are currently being used in the airports.

215
00:23:18,208 --> 00:23:27,008
And that is a challenge still, because we haven't invested enough in this country in terms of facilities for any deep technology product to go to market.

216
00:23:27,248 --> 00:23:28,368
Research is possible.

217
00:23:28,528 --> 00:23:34,208
For research, those 200 PhD students, each one of them is writing, let's say five papers, right?

218
00:23:34,350 --> 00:23:35,568
And there are 1000 papers.

219
00:23:35,790 --> 00:23:40,324
So when you count your publications, we will be top three, we will be top four in the country.

220
00:23:40,528 --> 00:23:55,728
But translating that science into a product, translating science into wealth, that requires a different level of engagement, that requires a different level of investments and facilities that we still lack right now in the country.

221
00:23:55,982 --> 00:23:57,248
Slowly things are getting better.

222
00:23:57,408 --> 00:24:02,048
I'm not saying that things are getting better, things are getting worse or anything, but we are very slow.

223
00:24:02,222 --> 00:24:08,208
We are not investing enough in the deep science kind of areas in terms of establishing those facilities.

224
00:24:08,494 --> 00:24:13,968
So, what takes abroad, right, you know, let's say five years to develop a technology.

225
00:24:14,158 --> 00:24:16,768
In India, it will be easily 10 years, right?

226
00:24:16,974 --> 00:24:19,088
The funding would also be one-tenth lower.

227
00:24:19,246 --> 00:24:28,128
So if you are able to wait for 10 years at one-tenth the funding, you can get a technology in these kind of areas to the market, right?

228
00:24:28,302 --> 00:24:33,408
But uh, but that kind of timelines, that kind of funding, you know, not everything can be done.

229
00:24:33,614 --> 00:24:50,980
And that's the reason why a lot of good science that's happening in our academia, you know, just doesn't go to the next level and doesn't get into the market, you know, primarily because of these delays, primarily because of lack of manufacturing facilities, you know, for these advanced technologies.

230
00:24:51,248 --> 00:24:54,688
I think that is a challenge that Indian Academia is facing right now.

231
00:24:54,848 --> 00:24:57,968
Otherwise, you have top researchers in the country.

232
00:24:58,448 --> 00:25:00,088
Look at the publications,

233
00:25:00,088 --> 00:25:03,568
But I think they'll challenge it again for the translation.

234
00:25:03,728 --> 00:25:06,928
The translation is where Indian Academia is struggling.

235
00:25:07,008 --> 00:25:12,964
It's not lack of scientific depth, it's not lack of talent or anything for that matter.

236
00:25:13,648 --> 00:25:13,968
Okay.

237
00:25:14,574 --> 00:25:15,888
Professor, I'd like to understand.

238
00:25:16,288 --> 00:25:17,248
let's narrow it down.

239
00:25:17,408 --> 00:25:25,796
It is tragic what you just mentioned, and unfortunately, it's a gaping hole that we have been facing for the last maybe four decades.

240
00:25:26,254 --> 00:25:28,388
Correct me if I'm wrong, it's more maybe longer.

241
00:25:28,686 --> 00:25:33,008
But I don't know why is it that we keep falling back into the same vicious cycle.

242
00:25:33,088 --> 00:25:34,820
Why is it that we don't tend to learn?

243
00:25:35,022 --> 00:25:37,008
Let me narrow it down further for you.

244
00:25:37,454 --> 00:25:43,648
1,27,500 crore rupees has been allocated as far as Semicon 2.0 is concerned.

245
00:25:43,808 --> 00:25:49,248
We have a Tata Dulera manufacturing facility coming into fruition.

246
00:25:49,408 --> 00:25:55,300
It's delayed right now, but well, hopefully it should be up and running sooner than later.

247
00:25:55,822 --> 00:26:08,484
Now, everything that you spoke about, if you were to just isolate it, sir, to this particular use case, how would you think would be a way that we'd be able to overcome the challenges?

248
00:26:08,768 --> 00:26:18,500
What would you like to go ahead and do to say, all right, we've learned from the past, we burnt our fingers, let's go ahead and try to resurrect ourselves in a way.

249
00:26:19,982 --> 00:26:30,500
You know, one thing when I started, my first company was in 2011, my second company was in 2018, Soil Sense, which is an agricultural sensor kind of a company.

250
00:26:30,768 --> 00:26:39,396
You know, the difference between 2011 to 18 is for a very, very long time in this country, public money cannot go to a private industry.

251
00:26:39,694 --> 00:26:40,848
It was a complete no,

252
00:26:40,848 --> 00:26:41,168
right?

253
00:26:41,582 --> 00:26:43,328
Government can give money to IITs.

254
00:26:43,488 --> 00:26:45,648
As a professor, I can raise money, right?

255
00:26:45,888 --> 00:26:49,328
I can go to a ministry and submit a proposal, defend and get money.

256
00:26:49,582 --> 00:27:01,008
But if the day I start a company, then the government money cannot come to a company because you know nobody is willing to sign on the dotted line saying then you know they said, but the moment you say a company, it is for profit.

257
00:27:01,248 --> 00:27:04,196
Now, why should public money go to a for-profit kind of a thing?

258
00:27:04,368 --> 00:27:08,448
And government cannot hold equity in any of these companies, you know, by nature of it.

259
00:27:08,622 --> 00:27:13,488
So, therefore, the question that many bureaucrats asked is you know, I don't want to take any risk, right?

260
00:27:13,728 --> 00:27:17,248
If you want to do raise private money, but we cannot fund you, right?

261
00:27:17,408 --> 00:27:22,128
For a deep tech kind of a company, the private investors were all looking for quick returns.

262
00:27:22,208 --> 00:27:29,968
They were not willing to wait for five years, six years, you know, with with with no guarantee for a success because the technologies are such that.

263
00:27:30,190 --> 00:27:33,488
And as a result, now nobody could take anything out to the market, right?

264
00:27:33,582 --> 00:27:43,488
Yeah, we were all happy writing papers, filing patents, right, with the hope that someday somebody will come, you know, take that patent and give us a billion dollars, which unfortunately never happened, right?

265
00:27:43,728 --> 00:27:51,888
But that is when government also, you know, we have been also saying the biggest challenge is the gap that exists between research and innovation, right?

266
00:27:52,110 --> 00:27:56,448
The research is, you know, you take money, you generate knowledge, you publish it.

267
00:27:56,608 --> 00:28:01,328
That part India has perfected very well because that has been the only focus of academia.

268
00:28:02,208 --> 00:28:11,488
Innovation is using that knowledge for public good, using that knowledge where people can you know buy the technology and then benefit from that, right?

269
00:28:11,758 --> 00:28:17,248
That part never received any funding from government or never any attention from government, right?

270
00:28:17,454 --> 00:28:20,688
As a result, now the innovation never happened in academia, right?

271
00:28:20,928 --> 00:28:26,084
Only for the past, I nano I was leading the nanotechnology mission.

272
00:28:26,288 --> 00:28:38,368
That was one of the first missions you know launched by government of India way back in 2002, 2003, kind of a time frame when former president Abdul Kalam, the late Abdul Kalam, was a president of India.

273
00:28:38,574 --> 00:28:42,448
There was a big meeting in Rashadabati Bhavan, a nano mission was launched.

274
00:28:42,734 --> 00:28:47,328
Professor CNR Rao, our pioneer in the nanotechnology area, led that.

275
00:28:47,488 --> 00:28:50,500
I was chairing the nanotechnology committee for a long time.

276
00:28:50,702 --> 00:28:52,708
There, you know, this was a challenge.

277
00:28:53,038 --> 00:28:55,808
And there we said, now we have done enough research.

278
00:28:55,968 --> 00:28:59,568
India was third in the world for nanotechnology research.

279
00:28:59,728 --> 00:29:03,408
Even now, India ranks third in the world for nanotechnology research.

280
00:29:03,598 --> 00:29:06,128
So we went back to the government and said, what next now?

281
00:29:06,608 --> 00:29:22,596
Now we have these technologies, we have the understanding, we have developed prototypes, we want to start, and no industry was willing to take the technology and develop any products because Indian industry is all service-oriented industries, pure grass-level manufacturing kind of industries.

282
00:29:22,798 --> 00:29:28,048
They were not taking any interest in any of these nanotechnology kind of products.

283
00:29:28,270 --> 00:29:31,460
So we went back to government and said, no, this is a challenge we are facing.

284
00:29:31,648 --> 00:29:33,968
Industry is not interested in what we are doing.

285
00:29:34,158 --> 00:29:38,768
And for us to start a company, the day we start a company, then you cannot fund us, right?

286
00:29:38,926 --> 00:29:40,768
How would we ever overcome this challenge?

287
00:29:41,168 --> 00:29:43,328
We tried and tried, nothing happened.

288
00:29:43,408 --> 00:29:48,768
We could not do anything you know, in the nano mission where nano mission could fund the projects, right?

289
00:29:49,006 --> 00:29:56,528
But if you see the recent quantum mission, which was launched you know, just about two years ago, quantum mission started with funding startups.

290
00:29:56,974 --> 00:30:00,452
They have given funding of 20 to 30 crores for startups.

291
00:30:00,750 --> 00:30:09,856
What in the AI mission they did recently, Sarvam receiving that kind of money from a government agency was unthinkable, you know, five years ago.

292
00:30:10,016 --> 00:30:10,336
Right?

293
00:30:10,574 --> 00:30:14,176
These are all things that have happened in the last three to four years.

294
00:30:14,336 --> 00:30:18,336
And thanks that, you know, we were able to learn from our earlier mistakes.

295
00:30:18,478 --> 00:30:22,256
And now government has started to get involved in funding these startups.

296
00:30:22,416 --> 00:30:26,416
We said you take equity, but government said, you know, we are not profit making.

297
00:30:26,496 --> 00:30:29,936
We cannot even hold any equity in any of these companies we fund.

298
00:30:30,254 --> 00:30:31,616
So that they had no mechanism.

299
00:30:31,696 --> 00:30:37,616
As a result, you know, the entire nanomission, though India was nowhere on the scene, was not even in the top 10.

300
00:30:37,838 --> 00:30:42,468
Thanks to Nanomission, we went to top three in the world when it comes to generation of knowledge.

301
00:30:42,656 --> 00:30:46,576
But no major products came out, no major technologies came out.

302
00:30:46,736 --> 00:30:49,216
Nobody could really use the technology, right?

303
00:30:49,376 --> 00:30:52,164
That was a mistake you know, that was happening at that time.

304
00:30:52,366 --> 00:30:57,936
But thanks to the recent five years, the last five years, government has learned from those mistakes.

305
00:30:58,126 --> 00:31:05,536
Government money going to companies has become a norm now, at least to the startups, if not to you know the bigger companies, right?

306
00:31:05,616 --> 00:31:07,936
And uh and now you are seeing the result of that.

307
00:31:08,142 --> 00:31:09,536
I think you know it's a good sign.

308
00:31:09,646 --> 00:31:15,616
And even quantum, I am very happy that they started with the with funding the quantum companies, right?

309
00:31:15,696 --> 00:31:18,656
And then started funding the academic research and all that.

310
00:31:18,816 --> 00:31:22,052
I would have liked exactly the same thing for the semiconductor mission.

311
00:31:22,318 --> 00:31:28,656
The India semiconductor mission where 10% of that money, right, would be like 10,000 crores.

312
00:31:28,976 --> 00:31:36,016
Now you say that 10,000 crores of money will go towards funding semiconductor startups in academia, right?

313
00:31:36,176 --> 00:31:44,896
And establishing facilities, you know, for uh for these startups to use in terms of manufacturing, right, would have made all the difference, right?

314
00:31:45,134 --> 00:31:48,416
But ISM 1 had zero money for academia, right?

315
00:31:48,654 --> 00:31:54,016
And ISM 2 now is talking of funding academia, but it hasn't actually started, right?

316
00:31:54,222 --> 00:32:01,136
Much of the ISM basically went as subsidies for these big industries to you know come and establish facilities.

317
00:32:01,326 --> 00:32:08,816
But I would have liked 10% of ISM, both ISM 1 and ISM 2, where academia strongly participating, right?

318
00:32:08,976 --> 00:32:17,136
And the startups that are coming out of these institutions receiving funding from the government of India along with access to facilities.

319
00:32:17,390 --> 00:32:22,816
Just a funding will not do, even if you give me 10 crore rupees, right, as just for my startup.

320
00:32:22,976 --> 00:32:32,816
But if I don't have the facilities in the country, with 10 crores, you can't really get anything done from any commercial foundry outside if your technology is proprietary, right?

321
00:32:33,006 --> 00:32:38,016
If it is a generic VLSI kind of a technology, the foundryless model is very common.

322
00:32:38,254 --> 00:32:44,336
But if your technology is highly IP protected, uses a process which is very unique, right?

323
00:32:44,496 --> 00:32:51,936
Somebody has to establish that process for you and manufacture based on your IP and give you back the process wafers.

324
00:32:52,256 --> 00:32:54,976
That process happens to be very, very expensive.

325
00:32:55,136 --> 00:33:03,296
And no Indian startup in this space can actually rent a commercial foundry to make their own proprietary process implemented.

326
00:33:03,376 --> 00:33:05,444
That has to be done within the country.

327
00:33:05,582 --> 00:33:11,696
And that is where you know there is at least one facility India has been able to establish in IASA Bangalore.

328
00:33:11,856 --> 00:33:16,336
Though there are facilities in IIT Bombay, IIT Delhi, the two institutions I am closely involved.

329
00:33:16,656 --> 00:33:20,016
But the Tata Dulera facility as well, sir, which is coming around.

330
00:33:20,096 --> 00:33:26,496
Uh, do you would you kind of at some level or the other count that also a part of the entire ecosystem?

331
00:33:27,086 --> 00:33:27,960
Will that assist?

332
00:33:29,102 --> 00:33:32,496
but those facilities can only execute standard processes.

333
00:33:32,736 --> 00:33:33,028
Correct.

334
00:33:33,696 --> 00:33:44,416
If you have a very unique process flow for your technology, a commercial foundry will look at it as a contamination, and they are not willing to then change their entire process.

335
00:33:44,622 --> 00:33:55,960
because every foundry can handle only certain types of materials, and you will not be able to introduce a material for your application, which could become a contamination for other process.

336
00:33:56,302 --> 00:34:06,976
So these foundries need to be very specialized, and these foundries need to be, you know, the contamination is a big concern in any foundry of semiconductors and nanotechnology.

337
00:34:07,214 --> 00:34:13,295
So therefore, these have to be small, small foundries, but meant for that particular process.

338
00:34:13,456 --> 00:34:25,056
You may be able to do one or two other things, but a dolera kind of a foundry, they will not allow you know, something which can contaminate their other, you know, money earning kind of technologies and processes.

339
00:34:25,262 --> 00:34:29,216
So these have to be you know, in association with academia, right?

340
00:34:29,454 --> 00:34:34,335
Small foundries, very specific focus, but multiple such foundries in academic institutions.

341
00:34:34,830 --> 00:34:36,480
And that is what is required.

342
00:34:36,750 --> 00:34:51,364
And that will change particularly, you know, I am one of the strongest believers that sensors and sensor research, the IOT kind of technologies, is where India has a huge potential.

343
00:34:51,679 --> 00:35:00,388
And that is going to be a trillion dollar market in the AI era because all physical AI, right, needs to gather information.

344
00:35:00,622 --> 00:35:06,884
The AI will do computation, but it has to gather information from wherever it is available from its surroundings.

345
00:35:07,120 --> 00:35:14,560
All of those sensors are India can be a good market, good uh India has a potential to develop these technologies.

346
00:35:14,640 --> 00:35:18,320
And for example, you know, skin, electronic skin for these robots.

347
00:35:19,054 --> 00:35:28,880
There should be at least 20 Indian institutions which must be working on electronic skin for robots, which are touch-sensitive, which can sense ambient conditions and all of that.

348
00:35:29,040 --> 00:35:35,360
There is not a single Indian academic institution which is even working on anything needed for the humanoids, right?

349
00:35:35,520 --> 00:35:43,280
We are still talking about AI competition and all that, but the next big revolution is going to be the robotics, the humanoids, right?

350
00:35:43,440 --> 00:35:48,320
And they are going to learn from our actions and perform those actions.

351
00:35:48,400 --> 00:35:51,600
And that is going to be even for defense applications, right?

352
00:35:51,790 --> 00:35:54,880
China is already talking of building a robotic army, right?

353
00:35:54,960 --> 00:35:59,680
I mean, these humanoids going and you know attacking people is all what is going to happen.

354
00:35:59,760 --> 00:36:03,204
And I think that is where India has a huge potential.

355
00:36:03,440 --> 00:36:07,120
And we have not even gotten into the physical AI space right now.

356
00:36:07,280 --> 00:36:11,556
We are still talking of you know, the AI and competition, and that is where we are stuck.

357
00:36:11,886 --> 00:36:19,600
India needs to invest a lot more in academic research into the academic institutions, irrespective of whether they are private, public.

358
00:36:19,822 --> 00:36:21,920
You just fund where the talent is, right?

359
00:36:22,126 --> 00:36:23,844
With very specific deliverables.

360
00:36:24,160 --> 00:36:25,840
And the talent is there.

361
00:36:26,062 --> 00:36:34,480
You know, we need to also scale up these institutions because with because of lack of funding, what has happened is Indian scientific base are also remained small.

362
00:36:34,800 --> 00:36:40,880
If you look at any of the recent reports, India has about 300 scientists per million population.

363
00:36:41,520 --> 00:36:44,000
Just around 300 scientists per million population.

364
00:36:44,320 --> 00:36:49,440
US would have something like close to 5,000 scientists per million population.

365
00:36:49,680 --> 00:36:53,280
China would have something like 2,000 scientists per million population.

366
00:36:53,440 --> 00:36:57,920
China has, same size, five times more scientists per million population.

367
00:36:58,190 --> 00:37:03,520
So even if government wants to invest money, it is the same thousand people.

368
00:37:04,000 --> 00:37:05,600
how much more money can you give them?

369
00:37:05,806 --> 00:37:07,716
So you need to build capabilities too.

370
00:37:07,920 --> 00:37:21,092
You need to expand our institutions, you need to have more campuses for IITs, you need to have private institutions receiving money indirectly from government for research grants, also expanding their research base.

371
00:37:21,358 --> 00:37:33,476
As a country, you know, we need to expand our research base so that we can get into developing these technologies, manufacturing things at scale, and then become the world's hub for at least some of these technologies.

372
00:37:33,680 --> 00:37:35,200
We will not be able to do everything.

373
00:37:35,520 --> 00:37:45,280
But at least for some critical technologies, you know, we need to have those proprietary, you know, kind of IP that we should be able to leverage going forward.

374
00:37:45,360 --> 00:37:50,404
Otherwise, without that leveraging capacity, you know, we will always be taken for granted.

375
00:37:51,406 --> 00:37:57,156
You know, Professor, it's wonderful that you've actually been so forthright with your criticism.

376
00:37:57,390 --> 00:38:06,596
